# P, the operation index, as a relation of its own. One row per operation a filing declares.
#
# The other conformed dimension
#   It owes a file for the same reason `layer` does. `draw` and `induction` are both P x L fact
#   tables, so a question about operations asked through an entry table gets the operations that
#   happen to have entries rather than the operations that exist. The two coincide only because
#   `pm:Operation` requires at least one draw or induction, which is a fact about the grammar that
#   no query states.
#
# A dimension needs a file to be a left operand
#   Without one, no law in algebra/ could govern a count of operations, and diagrams/roster.sqlc's
#   `tasks` law would have nothing to count against. Same repair as composition/carriable.sqlc and
#   composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc: the difference needs a named left operand.
#
# P carries the crossing
#   The crossing is an attribute of an operation and not an entry of anything.
#   `pm:Operation/foreignId` is at most one per operation, so it sits on the dimension the way
#   `part_regime` sits on a part. `entries/notation_references.sqlc` is the restriction to the
#   operations that file one, and it is a σ over this rather than a second read of `pm.operation`,
#   so the two cannot drift about what an operation is.
#
# `foreign_absent` as text
#   It is cast to text for `diagrams/searches.sqlc`'s reason: an emitter reads this, and a Postgres
#   enum has no built-in mapping on the Rust side. The type still guards the INSERT, which is where
#   a wrong word has to be caught. `epistemics/absences.sqlc` casts it back.
-- pm:Operation, keyed (filing, label), with the notation position or the reason there is none.
SELECT o.filing, o.label, o.foreign_notation, o.foreign_id, o.foreign_absent::text AS foreign_absent
FROM pm.operation o
